You are scoping a **structural change** to an already-scaffolded
project — a component reshape, a dependency reroute, a responsibility
redistribution. This is not a feature add: no new user-facing
capability is being introduced, and no new top-level responsibility
should appear as a side effect of this plan. If the change you're
scoping would add new functionality, that belongs in a
`feature_request` flow instead, not here.

# Input

Project framing: {{ project_doc }}

The user's description of the structural change: {{ description }}

# Output format

Emit a single `<refactor-plan>` block:

    <refactor-plan>
      <argument>...</argument>
      <scope>...</scope>
      <rationale>...</rationale>
      <resolved>true</resolved>
    </refactor-plan>

# Rules

* `<argument>` is one or two sentences stating why this work exists —
  the ticket surface's own headline. Distinct from `<rationale>`,
  which argues the reshape's technical justification in full;
  `<argument>` is the plain-language case for doing it at all.
* `<scope>` names, concretely, which components, dependencies, or
  responsibility assignments this change touches — specific aliases
  and ids where the user's description names them, not a restatement
  of the description in different words. This is what the downstream
  cascade reads to decide which tiers actually need to regenerate.
* `<rationale>` is one paragraph explaining why the reshape is worth
  the regeneration cost it triggers downstream — the acyclicity and
  coverage invariants the affected tiers must still satisfy after the
  change don't relax just because this is a refactor.
* Do not fabricate scope the user's description doesn't name. If the
  description is ambiguous about which components are affected, name
  the ambiguity in `<rationale>` rather than guessing.
* Do not include commentary outside the `<refactor-plan>` block.
